  "query_preview": "../Friday through Monday/...Active weather will continue \ninto the long term forecast as multiple systems move into the Gulf \nof Alaska. At the end the work week, a weak short wave moves into \nthe panhandle, as the ridge to the southwest of the area weakens. \nThe will bring light to moderate rain to the northern panhandle \nFriday, spreading into the southern panhandle by Saturday morning. A \nstronger low pressure system will then move into southeast into the \ngulf Saturday into Sunday. There is still some differences on where \nthe location of this low will be over the gulf. If it is farther \noffshore, rain amounts will be lower and less heavy. If the low \nmoves slightly closer to the panhandle, overall rain amounts will be \nslightly more, especially for the southern panhandle. Many of the \nsolutions do not bring super strong gulf winds, with wind speeds \nremaining around 20 to 30 kts Sunday. Confidence and model agreement \ngreatly decreases after the Sunday system, but the overall pattern \nof onshore flow looks the most likely to continue. \n\nTemperatures through the long term period remain near average with \nhigh temperatures in the mid to high 60s. There are indications that \nareas near Hyder will see temperatures into the mid to high 70s on \nFriday. Other southern areas could also see some warmer temperatures \nFriday as well, but not as warm as Hyder. The main indicators of \nthis is continued dry weather along with 850 temperatures between 12 \nto 15 C. The limiting factor will be the amount of cloud cover that \nthey receive as the low pressure system moves into the gulf bringing \nmore moisture into the area.",
